In article <334D5566.231B@vu.union.edu>, Rob Hagopian <Rob.Hagopian@vu.union.edu> writes:
>
>We have an RZ77 DAT Changer hooked up to our Dec 3000/700 with OSF1

So what do you really have?  We (Digital) use RZ names for rotating
disks.  We use TZ names for tapes.  We have never built an RZ77 disk
or a TZ77 tape.  The closest thing to that name is a TZ877, which is
DLT family TZ87 drive with a 7 slot loader.

>v3.2. Unfortunatly, I can't figure out how in the heck to change the
>tapes in the drive.
>	The manual for the changer claims that sequential and random access is
>possible for the drive, but much looking has turned up no individual
>commands to send those commands to the tape drive.

All of the loaders that we've sold that support sequential mode,
will do the appropriate thing when you take the tape offline.  On
DEC OSF/1 or Digital UNIX use "mt offline".  If you do have a
TZ877 or one of the other 7 slot loaders, you'll need to have the
front panel key in the right position.  The "2:30" position is
the right one, though I forget what the icon is.

For random access you need something to send SCSI-2 medium changer
commands.  If your loader is old enough that it didn't come with
a copy of the Media Robot Utility, you can order this since it is
the least expensive way to get this sort of control.  None of the
base system tape utilities have a clue what to do with random or
sequential loaders, bu tthere is a fair amount you can do with
"mt" and MRU.

The license numbers for MRU are:

QM-50TAA-AA - Software license only.
QB-50TAA-SA - License and CDROM media

