Adrian Burd wrote:
> 
> Greetings,
>           I am having a problem with the DEC Ladebug debugger and
> being new to this program I have no clue as to what is going on.
> We are running version 3.0.9 with DU3.2 on a DEC Alpha 3000. If I
> start the ladebug program with the program I am working on I get the
> following:
> 
>    [124] Src_nnes> decladebug steady
>    Welcome to the DECladebug Debugger Version 3.0-9
>    ------------------
>    object file name: steady
>    Reading symbolic information ...done
>    Warning: illegal aux index 63 for file #61 fit.f
>    Segmentation fault
> 
> The code is compiled with the -g option and the code fit.f is a
> standard routine from Numerical Recipes. Does anyone have a clue as to
> what "illegal aux error 63" is?
> 
> Many thanks.
> 
> Adrian
> 


  The message you are receiving indicates one of two things -
  either the debug symbol table is incorrect or there is a 
  problem with the ladebug debugger. What version of the compiler
  are you using ? I assume the system compiler that shipped with
  DU V3.2 ? The debug symbol table on UNIX has been greatly enhanced
  over the last few years, so the more recent the compiler, the
  more robust the symbol table will be. It is currently in an          
extremely stable state and we plan on keeping it that way as
  we add enhancements. Ladebug has been improving too, so you
  may want to upgrade your version of the debugger if you can.

  I haven't seen "illegal aux index" problems in a long time, so
  it is highly likely that upgrading your software will solve the
  problem.

  If you would like me to verify that the problem is resolved,
  send me some email and I will take a look.


  Bob Monteleone
  UNIX symbol table maintainer






