Otis Gospodnetic wrote:
> 
> Hi,
> 
> I recently tried installing MySQL on an Alpha running OSF1 v4.0.  It didn't
> work and MySQL authors told me I should really try to get OSF threads
> working.
> 
> Since I'm a few hundred miles away from the Alpha in questions, and all its
> manuals, I was wondering if anyone could maybe try explaining what I would
> need to do to this Alpha to get OSF Threads working on it (why wouldn't
> they be working in the first place?).

The first question is, what do you (or they) mean by "OSF threads"? By
"OSF1 v4.0", I assume that you mean "Digital UNIX 4.0". We stopped using
the name "DEC OSF/1" when X/Open made the UNIX trademark openly
available, and I doubt that there's an "OSF/1 v4.0", which, if such a
thing existed, would be a reference release directly from OSF, having
nothing to do with Digital.

My best guess is that they mean "DCE threads", which was a user-mode
implementation of draft 4 of the POSIX standard, developed by Digital
for the OTHER half of OSF, DCE. That package is a remote ancestor of the
modern Digital UNIX 4.0 thread package. For compatibility reasons, we
have retained the original draft 4 "DCE thread" API along with the
modern POSIX thread API. Most old DCE thread binaries will "just run"
under Digital UNIX 4.0, as long as they were built correctly. We have
found a few obscure compatibility issues in the DCE thread "wrappers"
that have been fixed with patches, so you should make sure that the
system is up-to-date.

Both the POSIX thread and "legacy" DCE thread interfaces are standard
and unavoidable parts of the base Digital UNIX kit, so you've got them
unless someone deliberately dismantled them from your system. (Of
course, you may not have the header files, unless you've got the
programming environment installed -- that might be relevant if their
installation tries to compile anything.)

If they mean anything else, I can't help to interpret it.

